  # Data
  
  [Download data on price houses](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/yasserh/housing-prices-dataset) and set it up in your working environment.

Load libraries (`tidyverse` and `effects`), and read the data in.

```{r}
library(tidyverse)
library(effects)

housing <- read.csv('')
```


# Question 

> 
  
  
  ## Distribution of price
  
  What is the distribution like for the house price variable? (plot a histogram)

```{r}
housing |>
  ggplot(aes(x = price)) +
  geom_histogram()


```



## Distribution of price x mainroad

What is the distribution of price across whether the house is near a main road or not? (plot a box plot)

```{r}
housing |>
  ggplot(aes(x = price, y = mainroad)) +
  geom_boxplot()
```

## Relatioship of price x area

What is the relationship of price and area? (plot a scatterplot)

```{r}
housing |>
  ggplot(aes(x = price, y = area)) +
  geom_point()


```

# Linear Model

Run a linear model on price (y) and any dependent variable you might find appropriate

```{r}
model_housing <- lm(price ~ stories+ mainroad + furnishingstatus + parking + basement + bathrooms + area +prefarea , data = housing)

summary(model_housing)
```

# What factors have an effect on house price?

The regression analysis rveals that several key factors significantly influence the housing prices. These include number of stories, availability of parking, number of bathrooms, presence of a basement, total area, and whether it's located in a preferred area or along the mainroad. Additionally, unfurnished homes tenf to significantly reduce the value, while semi furnished does not have a statistically significant impact.